#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078223 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d7a21aa-f2e3-5461-81a6-5cbd0f63c03d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term of an Arithmetic Series Given the Last Term and the Sum"}] :question/id #uuid "8d663147-d71b-5349-bb5b-c737d73d4d7e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4206" :question/problem "If the sum of the first $20$ terms of an arithmetic series is $S_{20}=560$, and the last term is $a_{20}=66$, what is the first term of the series?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the last term of our series. So, to find the first term, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series in the form\n\n$$\nS_N = \\dfrac{N}{2}(a_1+a_N)\n$$\n\nwhere $a_1$ is the first term, $a_N$ is the last term, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nSubstituting $a_{20}=66$, $N=20$, and $S_N=560$ into the formula above and solving for $a_1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n560 &= \\frac{20}{2}(a_{1} + 66) \\\\\n560 &= 10(a_{1} + 66) \\\\\n\\frac{560}{10} &= a_{1} + 66 \\\\\n56 &= a_{1} + 66 \\\\\na_{1} &= -10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078224 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f9251ee-4d19-5074-a2a3-07e13149260f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term of an Arithmetic Series Given the Common Difference and the Sum"}] :question/id #uuid "9e118fb4-4787-5ed1-9ab9-13cfce102726" :question/math-academy-id "e-4207" :question/problem "What is the first term of an arithmetic series if the common difference is $d=2$, and the sum of the first $50$ terms is $S_{50}=2\\,150$?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the common difference of the series. So, to find the first term, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series in the form\n\n$$\nS_N = \\dfrac{N}{2}\\big(2a_1+(N-1)d \\big)\n$$\n\nwhere $a_1$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nSubstituting $N=50$, $d=2$, and $S_N=2\\,150$ into the formula above and solving for $a_1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2150 &= \\frac{50}{2}(2a_{1} + (50 - 1)(2)) \\\\\n4300 &= 50(2a_{1} + 98) \\\\\n\\frac{4300}{50} &= 2a_{1} + 98 \\\\\n86 &= 2a_{1} + 98 \\\\\n- 12 &= 2a_{1} \\\\\na_{1} &= -6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078225 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "07a3dfec-3f7b-5820-9be5-12bf4acd5a3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term of an Arithmetic Series Given a Term and the Sum"}] :question/id #uuid "4de8d7af-50a7-5e9d-8f5d-908d6a06e174" :question/math-academy-id "e-4208" :question/problem "Find the first term of an arithmetic series if its $11$th term is $29$ and the sum of the first $16$ terms of the series is $384$." :question/worked-solution "We are given one term of the series. So, to find the first term, we use the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series in the form\n\n$$\nS_N = \\dfrac{N}{2}\\left(2a_1+(N-1)d \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $a_1$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nSubstituting $N = 16$ and $S_N = 384$ into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{N} &= \\frac{N}{2}(2a_{1} + (N - 1)d) \\\\\n384 &= \\frac{16}{2}(2a_{1} + (16 - 1)d) \\\\\n384 &= 8(2a_{1} + 15d) \\\\\n\\frac{384}{8} &= 2a_{1} + 15d \\\\\n48 &= 2a_{1} + 15d\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we can write an equation for the $11$th term of the arithmetic series and substitute in its known value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= a_{1} + (n - 1)d \\\\\na_{11} &= a_{1} + (11 - 1)d \\\\\n29 &= a_{1} + 10d\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{29 = a_{1} + 10d; 48 = 2a_{1} + 15d\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we multiply the first equation by $-2$ and add the result to the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-58 &= -2a_{1} - 20d \\\\\n48 &= 2a_{1} + 15d \\\\\n- 10 &= -5d \\\\\nd &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $d = 2$ into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n29 &= a_{1} + 10(2) \\\\\n29 &= a_{1} + 20 \\\\\na_{1} &= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d7a21aa-f2e3-5461-81a6-5cbd0f63c03d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term of an Arithmetic Series Given the Last Term and the Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f84a4da1-19dc-5ad4-8354-348f750971a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f84a4da1-19dc-5ad4-8354-348f750971a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232843} :answer-field/id #uuid "b09624b5-b584-5ff3-89f8-3e364ab73158" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075693}] :question/id #uuid "895e7cfd-35af-5059-ba6b-4107204ac08f" :question/math-academy-id "q-233787" :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nIf the sum of the first $50$ terms of an arithmetic series is $S_{50} = 600$, and the last term is $a_{50} = 21.8$, then the first term of the series is $a_{1} =$ {{field-1}}.\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"finding-the-first-term-of-an-arithmetic-series-when-the-last-term-is-not-given\"></a>" :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "07a3dfec-3f7b-5820-9be5-12bf4acd5a3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Term of an Arithmetic Series Given a Term and the Sum"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab0cc772-bbe7-5a4c-8513-7cadd3009f2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab0cc772-bbe7-5a4c-8513-7cadd3009f2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232844} :answer-